course: dictionary
lesson: 697
title: Banknotes and money words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A banknote is a piece of paper or plastic money printed by a country's bank. Coins are the metal money, and banknotes are the flat money you can fold into a wallet.
explain: In maths money problems, banknotes often stand for bigger amounts than coins. Look for the clues: the word sits near numbers like £5 or $10, and a note is paper or plastic, not metal.
explain: Two places, two names. In the UK people say «note», and in the USA and Canada they say «bill». A «$20 bill» and a «£20 note» are both banknotes, so if you meet «bill» in an American story, check the context to see whether it means money.
explain: Read this context sentence: «Studies have found that banknotes and coins can carry more germs than a toilet seat, which is cleaned often, so washing hands after handling money is sensible.» The clues are germs and washing hands, so banknotes are money that touches many hands, and washing your hands after handling it is a sensible habit.
fact: A banknote is printed by a country's bank.
fact: Banknotes have special pictures, threads and holograms, so they are hard to copy.
fact: «Fake banknotes» are copies made to trick people; fake means not real.
fact: In the UK people say note; in the USA and Canada they say bill.
fact: «Plastic banknotes» are still banknotes, so the word covers both paper and plastic.
exercise: Which of these is a banknote? | A 10p coin | A metal key | *A £10 note | A bag of sweets
exercise: Americans say «$20 bill» for a banknote. What do people in the UK call it? | Cheque | *Note | Coin | Card
exercise: What does «fake banknotes» mean? | Notes from a school game | A real note from the bank | Plastic cards for shops | *Copies made to trick people
test: What makes banknotes hard to copy? | Big numbers printed on the front | *Special pictures, threads and holograms | Hard metal edges
test: In a maths money problem, which is usually worth the most? | A handful of 1p coins | A pile of 5p coins | *A £20 banknote | A small bag of 2p coins
exam: Which sentence uses banknote correctly? | *I paid for the bread with a £5 banknote. | The banknote rained on the grass. | I ate my banknote for lunch.
